ly Basic-and-reversed because the list is scrolled 50px, and Basic row is behind the app bar/search bar (clipped), and it's truly there. In the \"reversed\" clear case, rows started at 340 (no scroll) with and-reversed first — that can't be explained by scroll since 340 is the standard first-row position. Unless the list's top padding changed.\n\nHmm, so maybe the issue is the list scrolled to the first *previously filtered* item and kept it at top (i.e., scroll-to-position of list item, adjusting adapter). Some list implementations preserve scroll anchor. Filtering \"reversed\" leaves 2 items; clearing shows 6; the anchor remains the and-reversed item at the top viewport, with Basic above out of view. That's plausible RecyclerView behavior: when items are re-inserted above, the anchor item keeps its screen position, so if it was at top (340), after clearing it stays at 340, pushing Basic above the viewport (out of screen). That's not \"missing from data\" — just scrolled such that the first row is above and inaccessible by scrolling up? You could scroll up to reveal Basic. In the screenshot state, the search bar overlaps? Hmm.\n\nIf it's scroll anchoring, the developer couldn't reproduce maybe because they scrolled? No, they just followed steps and maybe saw all rows because their list height/scroll position differed? Hmm.\n\nActually wait: is the list scrollable at all? Six rows each ~219px = 1314px, fits in the screen (2400 tall, list area from 240 to ~2100). So no scrolling needed normally; the list isn't scrollable when all 6 rows fit. But when filtered to 1 row, list becomes scrollable? No, content fits. Hmm, RecyclerView scroll offset shouldn't exist when content fits. But during filter, the list content shrinks to 1 row; if RecyclerView had a scroll offset, it would clamp to 0. Then clearing should reset to 0. So no.\n\nSo why the first row skipped? Maybe the adapter's first item is removed when the filter excludes it. This matches a genuine bug (filtering implementation removes non-matching items from the list permanently?). But then clearing \"basic\" (all 4 basic types match) kept all four Basic rows including the first, so nothing removed → correct. Filtering \"reversed\" removed Basic from the underlying all-types list? Then why would clearing restore the other types (type-in-answer, Cloze, IO) that also didn't match \"reversed\"? If filtering removed non-matching permanently, then after \"reversed\" only and-reversed and optional remain; clearing would show just those 2. But we saw 5 (all except Basic). So only Basic was removed. What's special about Basic? It's the first item. Hmm, and it has 7 notes (background count > 0). Filtering \"cloze\" (1 match at index 4): after clearing, Basic missing and and-reversed present — again only Basic removed.\n\nCould it be that Basic is hidden because its background note count update returns after the filter rebuild and the ListAdapter has stale position 0? Ugh.\n\nRegardless, from the user's perspective, the \"Basic\" note type disappears from the list after clearing a filter. The developer says they couldn't reproduce. Maybe the exact state matters: the counts must have been loaded asynchronously? We had entered Manage note types several times; Basic had 7 notes. Perhaps the developer's device had counts still computing, so the bug didn't manifest. Or maybe my current state included the extra note (Basic count 7 vs precondition 7? Actually precondition Basic notes = 7 exactly: 4+3. So same).\n\nWait — maybe the missing row isn't Basic but an artifact of my earlier action: I added a note and undid it, leaving the count at 7. Fine.\n\nLet me try to reproduce from a fresh app screen (force close/reopen? open_app doesn't reset). Let me do a clean reproduction: collapse search, back to deck list, re-enter Manage note types, then search \"reversed\", clear, observe. I'll do it now and capture screenshots to strengthen. If it reproduces, I'll re-report with clearer reproduction/