{"type": "thinking", "text": "Reproduced again with a 3s wait — so not timing-sensitive. The screenshot before showed clearly. Why couldn't dev reproduce? Possibly because they used a different filter query. The bug might only occur when the filter matches items such that the first *displayed* filtered item index differs? Hmm. Or maybe it needs the search query to be entered quickly with IME? Hmm.\n\nWait... maybe I'm misinterpreting the UI: maybe the missing row is actually scrolled above as I suspected, and the RecyclerView scroll offset is retained from the filtered state. The filtered state had 2 rows at positions y=340, 559; after clearing, if scroll offset were zero we'd see Basic at 340. But we see and-reversed at 340. If the list had scrolled by exactly one row height (219) during filter (why?), then after clearing we'd see Basic at 121 (behind the search bar) and and-reversed at 340. The search bar occupies up to about y=216, so Basic would be hidden behind search bar! That matches everything: the \"Basic\" row is *scrolled under the search bar*, and and-reversed appears at 340. And in the earlier \"cloze\" case, the scroll offset was such that and-reversed was at 290 (partially under search bar — its title hidden), and Basic above.\n\nHmm! But why would the list be scrolled down one row? When filter = \"reversed\" with 2 rows, the list content is short; maybe the RecyclerView anchored the previously-first-visible item (which was Basic at top) and after filtering, the item at the anchor... Hmm.\n\nCould the list be scrolled by 219? Check: can I swipe down (scroll up) to reveal Basic? If the row is just scrolled under the search bar, swiping down should reveal it. Let me test that! If Basic appears after scrolled, then it's not a data bug but a scroll-position/scrollability issue (still possibly a bug, but different: list not scrolled to top after filter cleared). If swiping doesn't reveal Basic, it's a data bug.\n\nSwipe down in the list area."}
