{"type": "thinking", "text": "qa_type gone, counts intact. Manage note types seems solid except the search-clearing scroll issue (rejected).\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the Manage note types bug is: the total note count includes notes of *deleted*? no.\n\nLet me reconsider the search issue: the developer said could not reproduce, but it's genuinely reproducible for me — the list retained a scroll offset, hiding the first row. Since they couldn't reproduce, maybe it's a device-size artifact (their screen bigger). Reporting again may annoy. But it is a real usability bug: after clearing a filter, the list stays scrolled so the first note type is not visible; a user could think it's gone. The screenshot evidence is clear. However, \"could not reproduce\" means they may have tested on similar device; odd.\n\nHold on, maybe the scroll offset isn't from filtering but because my keyboard is open and the list got scrolled when the IME appeared? When Search opens, the keyboard appears and the list area shrinks, causing the RecyclerView to keep the first item at top/scroll. After clearing, keyboard remains, list still scrolled. That's plausible: when the keyboard opens, the list content no longer fits, and the app scrolls to keep the focused item? Hmm, with all 6 rows and keyboard taking half the screen, the list height is ~1250 px; 6 rows × 219 = 1314 > 1250, so list scrolls a bit. Why would it scroll down by ~219? Maybe because IME opening caused the list to scroll down to keep the last row visible? Hmm. Then clearing filter doesn't reset to top because the keyboard is still open. That's a very plausible, device-size-dependent artifact: the developer's device maybe has a taller screen or smaller keyboard such that all rows fit above the keyboard.\n\nActually, in the screenshots, the keyboard covers the bottom half; the visible list area from ~216 to ~1500 (keyboard top). That's 1284 px; 6 rows × 219 = 1314 → slight scrolling. The first row could still be visible if scroll offset is small; but we observe Basic entirely hidden — offset ≥ ~219. Hmm.\n\nWait, in the corrupted state after clearing \"reversed\", visible rows at 340 (and-reversed), 559, 778, 997, 1216 (IO). The last row IO at 1216 is fully visible; the list viewport bottom is ~1500 (keyboard top at 1489 in screenshot). So all 5 rows fit; the list could have scrolled such that Basic is at 121 (behind toolbar). Offset ≈ 219. Why 219? Possibly because during filtering to 2 rows, scroll position clamped to 0; hmm.\n\nThe hidden row being exactly one row above suggests an off-by-one scroll anchoring bug tied to the adapter update (inserting items above the anchor without adjusting). It's real but minor; dev couldn't reproduce, so drop it.\n\nLet me now focus on the cloze Add card type report. I'm fairly confident it's a bug. But let me double-check the expected behavior by considering AnkiDroid's UI for Image Occlusion: IO note type is special; adding card types is surely invalid. Both show Add. I'll report on Image Occlusion or Cloze. Which is more clearly invalid? Cloze: \"cloze note types cannot have more than one card template\". Image Occlusion: even more clearly invalid (it's a special cloze-based type). The menu is identical for both.\n\nBut wait: could \"Add card type\" for cloze actually be legitimate in this AnkiDroid version? Let me test the result of saving a second template to Cloze and see if a cloze note with c1 generates 2 cards. If it generates 2 cards (one from Card 2 template), then the app treats cloze note types as template-based, which contradicts Anki's model but maybe AnkiDroid supports it. Let's actually test this end-to-end in a controlled way:\n\n1. Add Card 2 to Cloze, save.\n2. Add a Cloze note with text \"{{c1::hello}}\" in some deck.\n3. Check \"Cards:\" button listing and deck card count: if 2 cards expected? For cloze note with one cloze, Anki makes 1 card. With 2 templates, if AnkiDroid generates 2 cards, then the feature works (though cross-compatible with Anki? Anki desktop would probably also treat ord? Hmm).\n4. Delete the cloze note and the extra template afterwards.\n\nIf AnkiDroid's backend rejects multi-template cloze (unlikely; it allowed adding in editor), the save might fail.\n\nActually, the safest is to test and then clean up. But data changes to the note type (adding Card 2) affect the collection. We can delete Card 2 after testing. Should be okay.\n\nAlternatively, I can save Card 2 and immediately check whether Anki's card count for a new cloze note doubles. The confirmation matters for the report's expected behavior. Let's do it.\n\nSteps: go to Cloze card types (Manage note types → Cloze Edit cards), Add card type, Add, Save; then Add note with type Cloze, text {{c1::foo}}, deck QASub, save and see \"cards added\" message (1 or 2). If 2 cards added, then... hmm, that would mean cloze note types can have extra templates and generate per-template cards? But for cloze, one card per cloze per template? That would be a big feature change, unlikely intended.\n\nHmm wait, maybe this app build is based on a newer AnkiDroid (2.18?) where cloze note types CAN have multiple templates? Let me recall the latest Anki: In Anki 2.1.60+, cloze note types still only one template. In Anki's notetype model, \"cloze\" notetypes have templates list but only one; the editor's \"Add card type\" is hidden/disabled. So yes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the possibility that the planted bug in the card-template-editor menu is the *missing* \"Rename card type\" and \"Deck override\" for Cloze/Image Occlusion, not the extra Add. If the code was changed to \"build the menu the same way for every kind of note model\", then all models should have all entries, but cloze-specific hiding may have been intended. Which entries are type-dependent in AnkiDroid?\n\nLet me think about AnkiDroid 2.16 CardTemplateEditor source in more detail. I recall code in `CardTemplateEditor.java`:\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic boolean onPrepareOptionsMenu(Menu menu) {\n    super.onPrepareOptionsMenu(menu);\n    boolean isCloze = mModel.isCloze();\n    menu.findItem(R.id.action_add_card_type).setVisible(!isCloze);\n    menu.findItem(R.id.action_reposition).setVisible(!isCloze && mModel.getCardTypes().size() > 1);\n    menu.findItem(R.id.action_rename_card_type).setVisible(!isCloze);\n    menu.findItem(R.id.action_delete_card_type).setVisible(!isCloze && mModel.getCardTypes().size() > 1);\n    menu.findItem(R.id.action_browser_appearance).setVisible(!isCloze);\n    menu.findItem(R.id.action_deck_override).setVisible(!isCloze);\n    ...\n}\n```\nHmm, this is speculative memory from having read AnkiDroid code, but the pattern \"if cloze, hide most items\" rings true *except* Add... \n\nActually, I recall there is a known nuance: In AnkiDroid, for cloze notetypes the menu shows \"Add card type\" disabled? Let me think about user reports: \"AnkiDroid allows adding card types to cloze note types, which Anki desktop then complains about\" — I don't recall such.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the planted bug is exactly: \"For Cloze and Image Occlusion, the menu includes 'Add card type' whereas these types can only have one card template; before the refactor it was hidden.\" The phrase \"built the same way for every kind of note model\" maps directly: the code now constructs the same menu (all entries) for every note model, forgetting type-specific exclusions. And indeed the only obviously wrong entry is Add (since Rename/Deck override are absent, they must have been excluded deliberately by some other check? Hmm, inconsistent).\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the menu entries are built with the same *list* for every model, but each entry's visibility depends on conditions. Then Add's condition is wrong. Fine.\n\nLet me look for other anomalies in the menus we saw:\n- Basic (1 template): Add, Rename, Browser, Deck override(off), Insert field, Copy as Markdown, Restore to default. Missing \"Reposition\"/\"Delete\" (because 1 template — correct).\n- Basic (2 templates): Add, Reposition, Rename, Delete, Browser, Deck override(off), Insert field, Copy, Restore. Complete.\n- Cloze: Add, Browser, Insert field, Copy, Restore. Missing Rename and Deck override. If Rename/Deck override were incorrectly hidden due to the refactor, the developer wou", "truncated": true}
