{"type": "thinking", "text": "Press back (leaves editor without saving; no changes made since entering, so no confirmation). Then back to deck list, use overflow undo.\n\nAlso note: The dialog \"Cannot delete card type - Deleting this card type will leave some notes without any cards.\" for the Cloze notetype with 2 templates is itself interesting: it suggests the app thinks some notes' only cards are on Card 2. Maybe this is the actual seeded bug manifestation: after adding a card type to a Cloze note type, the existing notes' cards got *moved* to Card 2 (or the app can't delete because of ord confusion). Hmm. Let's inspect further AFTER cleanup? Not necessary.\n\nBut wait — maybe this explains why the dev couldn't reproduce my \"no card created\" claim: maybe cards WERE created for Card 2 (existing cloze notes' cards moved to Card 2?), and the deck counts didn't increase because no new card was added but the existing cards' template changed? Hmm. Card browser shows \"Cloze 1\" for their card types; that's cloze-number labeling, not template name. Maybe the browser column for cloze shows \"Cloze 1\" (card type = cloze number), so we can't tell which template. Ugh.\n\nIf existing cards were reassigned to Card 2, then the total card count wouldn't increase (consistent with our observation) — and that's a different bug: adding a card template to a Cloze notetype silently moved existing cards to the new template instead of creating new ones. Then the confirmation \"This will create 1 card\" was also wrong. And the deletion block makes sense: deleting Card 2 would leave notes without cards (because their cards now live on Card 2). Hmm! But would the app reassign? When you add a card type to a standard notetype, existing notes' new card is generated; existing cards stay on their templates.\n\nHmm, the \"Cannot delete\" guard likely uses `note.hasCards()`? Let me look at Anki behavior: In Anki desktop, deleting a card template prompts \"This will delete X cards. Are you sure?\" or if some notes would be left with no cards, it refuses: \"Cannot delete card type — some notes would be left with no cards.\" For our Cloze type with 2 templates and 2 notes: if each note has 1 card on template 0, deleting template 1 shouldn't leave them cardless. Unless the cards are on template 1!\n\nLet's test: In the Card Browser, the \"Card Type\" column for cloze cards showed \"Cloze 1\". Maybe for cloze, AnkiDroid displays \"Cloze 1\" for ord 0 cards and \"Card 2\" for template 1? Our cards show \"Cloze 1\" → likely template 0. Hmm, wait — maybe after adding the template, the note's cards were regenerated and ord changed? The display \"Cloze 1\" might mean cloze number 1 regardless of template.\n\nCould check the card's template by opening the card in the browser (preview?) — e.g., tap the card row to open the note editor; the \"Cards\" button in note editor might list which templates have cards? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, check whether the notes have 1 or 2 cards each via browser: filter search `note:qa_cloze`? The browser shows 13 cards total; if each cloze note had 2 cards, total would be 15. 13 = 7 Basic (7 cards: 4 Spanish + 3 French) + qa_rev1 2 + qa_subnote 2 + qa_cloze 1 + qa_cloze2 1 = 13. So each cloze note has exactly 1 card. So cards are template 0 (\"Cloze\") or template 1 (\"Card 2\")? Either way 1 card each.\n\nIf their single cards were on Card 2 (template 1), deleting template 1 would indeed leave notes without cards → blocked. That would mean adding template 2 caused the cloze note cards to move to template 1?! Weird but possible if Anki's cloze ord = template index? For cloze, maybe card ord = cloze number - 1 (0 for c1), which corresponds to template index 0. So cards ord 0 = template 0. Deleting template 1 shouldn't affect them. Unless AnkiDroid's deletion check is conservative: \"Cannot delete card type if the notetype is cloze\"? Hmm, maybe for cloze notetypes, deleting any template is only allowed if there are no notes with cards? Hmm.\n\nBut here's a thought: Maybe the deletion block is because the app thinks deleting template 1 would leave *notes without any cards* due to a bug in the check for cloze notetypes: e.g., it checks `notes each have a card with ord != templateIndex`; for cloze, cards' ord = 0 for each note. Deleting template 1: notes have card ord 0 != 1, so should be fine. Deleting template 0: notes' only card ord 0 == 0 → would block. So if the app blocked deleting template 1, maybe it's using the wrong index (0)? Or maybe the cards' ord is 1!\n\nHmm, maybe when adding a template to a cloze notetype, AnkiDroid assigns the new card to template 1 by regenerating cards, and deletes the old ord-0 cards? That would keep card count the same and explain the block. But then the new cards would use the Card 2 template content (\"{{cloze:Text}}<br>{{Back Extra}}\"), barely different. Not obvious visually.\n\nThis is a deep rabbit hole and the dev rejected the report. Let me revert and move on. I'll avoid further data alterations.\n\nRevert: back out of the editor, use undo. Note: we are in the editor without changes (we attempted delete but cancelled). Press back to Manage note types, back to deck list. Then overflow → Undo Add Note (qa_cloze2), then Undo Update Note Type (revert template). Then undo qa_cloze, qa_subnote, qa_rev1 additions one by one. Then delete subdecks.\n\nLet's start."}
